          "id": "SP_SERAPHS_NARICUS",
          "name": "Seraphs naricus",
          "rank": "SPECIES",
          "commonName": "",
          "lineage": "Seraphs",
          "subspeciesCount": 0,
          "description": "The shell is slender and cylindrical with a moderately pointed apex. The aperture is long and narrow. The labrum is thin and prosocyrt near the adapical end of the aperture, becoming opisthocline on the teleoconch. Distribution.? OLIGOCENE — Rupelian — Pakistan Bhagothoro Hill, Sind (Jung 1974). Remarks. This species is known only from one broken example. It is one of the earliest Seraphs, and is considered a transitional stage to Terebellum based in geological time and location (Jung 1974).",
          "sourcedFrom": "gbif",
          "wikipediaTitle": "gbif:4963528",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "id": "SP_SERAPHS_PETERJUNGI_CAZE_MERLE_PACAUD",
          "name": "Seraphs peterjungi Caze, Merle & Pacaud",
          "rank": "SPECIES",
          "commonName": "",
          "lineage": "Seraphs",
          "subspeciesCount": 0,
          "description": "The shell is moderately ovate and elongated, with the widest part being just below the adapical end of the aperture. The apex is moderately blunt. The surface of the shell is smooth with faint sinuous growth lines. The labial callus is well-developed and conspicuous. The labrum is not thickened and has a moderately quadrate profile. The columella is recurved backward near the base. The base of the labrum does not extend as far as the end of the columella. The anterior of the body whorl has fine axial grooves.",
          "sourcedFrom": "gbif",
          "wikipediaTitle": "gbif:9154494",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "id": "SP_SERAPHS_SUBCONVOLUTUS",
          "name": "Seraphs subconvolutus",
          "rank": "SPECIES",
          "commonName": "",
          "lineage": "Seraphs",
          "subspeciesCount": 0,
          "description": "The shell is cylindrical with a long, narrow aperture. The labial callus is thin in contrast to the labrum, which is slightly thickened and prosocyrt below the adapical end of the aperture. Above the adapical end of the aperture the labrum runs antispiral, reaching the apex after about half a volution. The columella is slightly bent backward near the base.",
          "sourcedFrom": "gbif",
          "wikipediaTitle": "gbif:4941018",
          "children": []
        },
